As one person responded, since this is a switched mode power supply
(SMPS) you will need a load to make it function correctly.  Trace
continuity from the output of the PS to the loads (disks, MB, etc.) If
you have verified the wiring is correct (then the loads are intact),
the most likely the PS is dead.
You could also cobble together a few  resistors of the appropriate
resistance to get at least 1 amp at the nameplate voltage (e.g. 5, 12,
-12vdc) (e.g. 5v*1a=3D5w) and hang them off of the power supply plug. I
don't know what all voltages the PS supplies but these are a likely
guess. I would make loads for as many output voltages as there are,
connect them all at the same time, and then measure the voltages.
(Disconnect the disks, mb, and any other load before doing this).
If this still results in 0 output voltage, the supply is hosed. There
are two general class of failures for SMPS: semiconductor devices in
the feedback ckt, and capacitors.  As a hunch, given the age, some of
the caps are probably shorted. If you have time/patience/skills you
could shotgun it and replace all the caps. Make sure the replacements
are high quality caps designed for smps (usually have a high ESR).
Having not seen one of these PS, but if they are like PC PS's. I'd
look for a new one.  A used one, having the same approximate age would
be likely to have weaker caps, and in short time would fail.
Given the above, I'd 'borrow' a known working good PS to try before
spending the time rebuilding one.
Then again, one of the loads may have killed the original PS and could
do the same to the new PS, so I would try loads one at a time, e.g.
one hd, then another, cd drive, MB, etc.)
Good luck
John


