Dead caps are a good guess. Spare PSUs seem to be available and might
save time but I haven't seen anyone with prices, which probably isn't
good news.

Not everyone has access to high wattage resistors to test a power
supply, but more people may have access to 12v light bulbs (from cars
or low voltage halogen units) which can with a bit of care be used to
similar effect, either alone or in series or parallel combinations.
Some improvisation may be required. Bear in mind that a halogen lamp
running at lower than its official rating has a lower resistance than
you would calculate from the rating (if that makes any sense), and
don't attempt to get too close to the PSU's rated output currents. You
probably don't want headlamp bulbs for variouis reasons, you want the
smaller ones.

I think the AlphaStation 400 PSU has the same voltage outputs as an
ATX PSU (+12, -12, +5, -5, +3.3) and the ratings are probably roughly
comparable with a decent 300W ATX PSU, at least for a quick test. Some
improvisation of connectors would be required.

The advice to add loads one at a time in case one of them is dubious
is very wise. Care is also needed if testing these parts in a
different box or with a different PSU (don't try them in the favourite
shiniest box, just in case), although I would be disappointed if an
output overload had killed a DEC PSU. Mind you by this stage this
wasn't really a DEC designed PSU, just a reputable commercial one.

This box was also sold by DEC's PC business unit with an Intel
processor card, perhaps under the name DECpc XL or DECpc Celebris XL,
I forget exactly which, but the point is the PSU should hopefully be
compatible (and might be cheaper if it's not got an "Alpha"
association).

Again, best of luck.


